I opened the challenge image and checked its properties. It was a PNG
file, but common image tools could not decode it properly. This
suggested that the image structure might be corrupted.
I started by examining the PNG structure. A PNG file contains chunks
such as IHDR, IDAT, and IEND. IHDR stores the image dimensions, IDAT
contains the compressed image data, and IEND marks the end of the image.
The file had an invalid IHDR CRC and broken chunk boundaries. I tried
tools such as pngcrush, ImageMagick, binwalk, and PCRT, but these
attempts did not directly reveal the flag.
Next, I examined the compressed IDAT data. The IDAT data did not
decompress correctly at first because a decoy IEND chunk had been
inserted inside the compressed data. Removing that 12-byte sequence
restored the compressed stream and allowed its integrity checks to pass.
The image still had an incorrect height value. The IHDR chunk said the
image was 724 × 800, but the recovered decompressed data contained 850
rows. I corrected the height to 850 and recalculated the IHDR CRC.
I then checked that the scanline filter bytes were valid and opened the
repaired image. The bottom 50 rows, which had been hidden by the
incorrect height, contained the flag.
